  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/32d2ecb4cc0fe71888d4ca89f0c5c493d0746ae9f97a9253ef42d69f45880086.png)\n\nWhat is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(9,4)$ and passes through the point $(-7,12).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(9,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x-9).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,12),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=12$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-4)^{2} & =4p(-7-9) \\\\ 64 & =4p\\cdot (-16) \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-4(x-9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =-4(x-9) \\\\ 16 & =-4(x-9) \\\\ -4 & =x-9 \\\\ x & =-4+9 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $5.$",
